Some programs are mine, some are not. I took some programs that I like and
I would not been able to write them better, like EEPROM 1. All the original descriptions
are there with links and information on who wrote them and how.

The ROM-EEPROM change is fully automatated by atmega and its interfaces. When int8_t preset_id
is greater than 7 its instantly switch mosfet connected to T0 fv-1 pin.

2. EEPROM

I dont have EEPROM programmer so i flashed every byte with python and arduino.
Both WriteToEEPROM.py and .cpp are for that matter. arduino is interface and python is to load and push programs.
Both SpinASM and SpinCAD Designer will compile hex files so to write to eeprom you must first convert that to bin.
I recommend using IntelHex module tobinfile function, I did it before writing to EEPROM but you can do this in just
one script as well.
If you dont have any programs on your own you can create a bank of presets with already existing presets and compile
them together in SpinASM IDE if preset is .spn or SpinCAD Designer if .spcd/.spbk

Schematic for how to wire this up is here
Arduino         EEPROM
A5          ->  SCL
A4          ->  SDA
GND         ->  Vss, WP, A0, A1, A2 for 0x50 ADDR
5V          ->  Vcc

3. Writing programs

Spin ASM is rather complex, and despite my and other creators' efforts to explain
the code simply, I cannot guarantee that you will grasp certain concepts and program
structures that easily, so I recommend using SpinCAD at the beginning. I didn't like it,
to be honest, and was interested in learning ASM anyway, so I chose the harder path,
I guess. In other words, if you just want to make a cheap multi-effect to play around,
go for SpinCAD, but if you are into DSP, low-level programming, or electronics,
you should chose ASM.

For better understanding of spin asm I suggest to look up datasheet for that and other
tutorials and sources. For that matter i highly recommend learning "regular" asm first
or at the same time

